\ The row-aware self-tail-call back-edge is a second route out of a bracketed splice, and it is
\ AOT-only: this combinator's body collapses to a bounded row, which the ordinary back-edge
\ declines. See `aot_row_tail_loop.1z` for the same shape without a definition.
\
\ The recursing branch defines a word, so the may-define analysis brackets it and the back-edge
\ has an open transient frame to pop before it jumps. A frame left open would still be on the
\ stack when `shadow-prelude` runs, and its definition would land there instead of in the entry
\ frame, so the dictionary-shadow guard's gate would not hold and the throw below would not
\ happen. One leaked frame is enough to lose it.
my-times: ( n quot -- ) [
  over 0 >
  [ helper: [ 1 ] ; dup 2dip swap -- swap my-times ]
  [ 2drop ]
  if
] ;

\ A word body is bare on every tier, so this definition targets whatever frame is topmost.
shadow-prelude: ( -- ) [ nip: ( -- y ) [ 0 ] ; ] ;

3 [ ] my-times

shadow-prelude
